=== MOTIVATION LETTER ===

Language: {{ letter_language }}
Candidate: {{ candidate_name }}

Goal:
Write a natural, professional motivation letter adapted to the role and company. The letter must sound like a real candidate, not a generic AI-generated application.

Output format:
Subject: concise, mentioning the role and the candidate name.

Then exactly 3 paragraphs separated by blank lines.
No bullet points.
No section titles.
No exaggerated enthusiasm.
No generic sentence that could apply to any company.

Length:
- French or English: {{ target_word_count | default("220-300") }} words.
- If the application field is short, prioritize clarity over completeness.

Paragraph 1:
Explain why the candidate is interested in the company and role.
Use only 1 or 2 visible anchors from the job offer or company context.
Focus on the company's real context, mission, product, industry, team, or role scope.
Avoid generic phrases such as "your company is innovative" unless supported by the offer.

Paragraph 2:
Explain the fit between the candidate profile and the role.
Use 2 or 3 strong proofs maximum from read-only profile experiences and selected projects.
Explain why each proof is relevant to the role missions.
Express technical evidence in clear recruiter-facing language without losing specificity.
For professional experiences, use past or clearly neutral wording only, such as "During my experience at [company]...", "At [company], I developed...", "Chez [entreprise], j’ai développé..." or "Lors de mon expérience chez [entreprise]...". Replace the placeholders only with employers from the selected evidence.
Do not write formulations such as "my current role", "currently at [company]", "dans mon poste actuel" or "actuellement chez [entreprise]" for professional experiences.
Projects marked current or ongoing may still be described as current work.
Do not reproduce or closely paraphrase CV bullets.
Avoid technology lists; mention a specific tool only when it is necessary to understand the proof.
Stay strictly grounded in selected evidence.

Paragraph 3:
Explain the concrete contribution the candidate could bring.
Connect the candidate’s expected contribution to one concrete priority from the role, company context, mission, product, team or responsibilities.
Make the paragraph specific enough that it could not be reused unchanged for another company.
Avoid stock openings or closings unless they are immediately connected to the offer context.
End with a brief, natural invitation to exchange; the invitation must not be the main content of the paragraph.

Evidence rules:
Only claim skills, tools, domains, or experience that are present in {{ supported_candidate_evidence }}.
A tool or method documented in a read-only experience or selected project may be mentioned even if it is absent from allowed_skills. Respect its allowed_claims and evidence_level. An offer requirement, related tool or provider name alone never proves the candidate used a specific technology or service.
If an offer requirement is not directly supported, use adjacent supported skills and concrete evidence instead.
Never mention gaps, missing experience, limited knowledge, learning needs, apologies, or defensive caveats.
Do not use phrases such as "je n'ai pas", "limited", "although", "despite", "I am still learning", or "ready to learn".

Tone:
Natural, precise, sober, and confident.
Adapted to HR readers: understandable, business-oriented, and not overly technical.
Show technical credibility through selected examples, not through jargon density.

Personalization:
The letter must clearly show why this company and this role specifically make sense for the candidate.
Use company-specific context, but avoid copying the job description.
Avoid overusing the company name.

Final check before output:
- Exactly 3 paragraphs.
- No bullet list.
- No unsupported claims.
- No defensive wording.
- No generic motivation.
- Clear link between company needs, candidate evidence, and expected contribution.

=== STYLE ===
The style guidance below applies only to generated fields. It never authorizes changes to the static experience descriptions.
Tone: {{ tone }}
Voice: {{ voice }}
CV language: English only. Keep cv_title and professional_summary in English. Experience text is inserted from the approved English profile.
CV title: write a stable, polished professional headline under the candidate's name. Lightly adapt it to the broad role family, but do not optimize it for job keywords. Keep it specific enough to signal the target positioning: avoid a bare generic role title when one broad natural qualifier is supported by both the profile and the role context; use the bare role only when any qualifier would be unsupported or awkward. Use broad role/domain positioning when natural, not tools or stack keywords. Do not copy the job title, describe tasks, or turn required skills, tools, missions or keywords into a title suffix. If the title needs a "for ...", "focused on ...", or "specialized in ..." clause to explain the offer, move that clause to professional_summary instead. Choose the simpler natural headline over a synthetic but more precise phrase. Broader supported work emphasis belongs in professional_summary; specific implementation technologies, tools and stack details belong in selected_skills.
Professional summary: write 1-2 dense, recruiter-facing sentences, max {{ max_summary_lines }} lines and max {{ max_summary_length }} characters. Ground it in summary_source, experiences and projects. Use profile evidence, allowed_claims and evidence_level to support any named method, model family, project or implementation technology, including terms absent from the display catalogue; do not enumerate skills as standalone content.
Make it concrete rather than generic:
  - Start from the same broad role family as cv_title, then add the offer-specific work emphasis here; do not force the exact cv_title phrase or job-title wording into the summary.
  - Mention 2-4 supported evidence areas from the profile when relevant to the offer, such as domain, data type, model family, workflow type, project context or evaluation scope.
  - Do not turn the summary into a stack inventory or keyword list; named technologies, methods or projects must be tied to concrete source-grounded work.
  - When using evidence from an ongoing or self_reported project, qualify it explicitly as ongoing/current work or omit it. Prefer narrow wording such as "ongoing 3D dental scan project" or "current work on 3D dental scan understanding"; avoid broad claims like "3D research settings", "experience across 3D research", or "expertise in 3D medical AI" unless verified by stronger evidence.
  - Do not claim technologies requested by the offer, production ownership, cloud ownership, leadership, mentoring, or senior scope unless directly supported by profile evidence.
  - Avoid generic phrases like "applied AI", "end-to-end AI workflows", "deeptech contexts", or "cross-functional R&D" unless immediately tied to concrete profile evidence.
  - Keep the opening role consistent with cv_title; for example, do not write "Data Scientist with..." under an "AI Engineer" title.
CV title self-check before output:
  - Would this headline look natural under the candidate name on a CV?
  - Is it specific enough without becoming a task phrase or keyword bundle?
  - Is it a professional label rather than a sentence, task description or keyword-coverage field?
  - Did you avoid treating cv_title or professional_summary as keyword-coverage fields, keeping specific implementation technologies, tools and stack details in selected_skills?
Experiences are read-only context. Do not output, rewrite, shorten, select or reorder them. The application inserts the approved profile descriptions automatically.
